# Ansible Notes


## Configuring Password-Less Setup
Not literally Password less we will create ssh keys and add configuration in ansible to use the secret key generated.

```
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -a 100 -f ~/.ssh/my-server-key -C "prod-server"
```

Copy the public key and add it to the Authorized keys file in the target server.

## Configure Ansible

```shell
python3 -m pip install ansible
```

Note: If you dont have pip, install it first

## First Steps
### Creating a inventory file

- You can create file in both ini format and yaml
- I prefer yaml

Here there is a format which needs to be followed for the inventory file. do check that.

```yaml
all:
    vars:
    children:
        [group]:
            vars:
            hosts:
                [your own hostname]
```

Sample
```yaml

all:
  children:
    webservers:
      vars:
        ansible_ssh_private_key_file : /home/ec2-user/.ssh/my-server-key
        ansible_user: ec2-user
      hosts:
        host1:
          ansible_host: 172.31.20.235
```

To verify your inventory file

```shell
ansible-inventory -i inventory.yaml --graph
ansible-inventory -i inventory.yaml --list # this more nicer
```

### Adhoc Commands

You can check more on the official website
https://docs.ansible.com/projects/ansible/latest/command_guide/intro_adhoc.html

```shell
ansible -i inventory.yaml -m ping all
ansible -i inventory.yaml -m shell -a "ls /" all
```


## Now lets see about playbooks

A Playbook is a YAML file that defines a series of actions to be executed on managed nodes. It contains one or more "plays" that map groups of hosts to roles

- Sample Playbook
```yaml
---

- hosts: webservers
  become: y
  tasks:
    - name: Install apache httpd
      ansible.builtin.yum:
        name: httpd
        state: present
        update_cache: yes
    - name: copy the sample file
      ansible.builtin.copy:
        src: ./index.html
        dest: /var/www/html
        owner: root
        group: root
        mode: '0644'
    - name : Start the service
      ansible.builtin.service:
        name: httpd
```

```shell
ansible-playbook -i inventory.yaml httpd-play/apache-play.yaml
```

## Roles

### Ansible Galaxy

So now, so far we played around and created plabook & adhoc commands, but if you got more tasks to be run and you want to reuse it this will get messy to be in a single file. 

To help with that we have Roles. It provides sttuctural way to maintain all these

```shell
ansible-galaxy role init httpd-test
```
The above command creates you a skeleton sturcture for the role

```
httpd-test
├── README.md
├── defaults
│   └── main.yml
├── files
├── handlers
│   └── main.yml
├── meta
│   └── main.yml
├── tasks
│   └── main.yml
├── templates
├── tests
│   ├── inventory
│   └── test.yml
└── vars
    └── main.yml

8 directories, 8 files
```

Deafaults ----> Are default variables which can be used accross the role

Files ----> You static content files

Handlers ---> Are kind of function, you can call them repeatedly based on action

meta ----> Metadata about the role

tasks ----> Your list of tasks

templates ----> You use templates(jinja2) when you want to dynamically edit a file based on run time and input vars

vars ----> This is the variables of your role


#### Now lets restructure our previous playbook into role

```yaml
# main.yaml

- hosts: webservers
  become: y
  roles:
    - httpd-test
```

```yaml
# httpd-test/tasks/main.yaml

---
# tasks file for httpd-test

- name: Install apache httpd
  ansible.builtin.yum:
    name: httpd
    state: present
    update_cache: yes
- name: copy the sample file
  ansible.builtin.copy:
    src: files/index.html
    dest: /var/www/html
    owner: root
    group: root
    mode: '0644'
  notify:
    - restart_httpd
```

```yaml
# httpd-test/handlers/main.yaml
---
# handlers file for httpd-test
- name : start_httpd
  ansible.builtin.service:
    name: httpd
    state: started
- name : restart_httpd
  ansible.builtin.service:
    name: httpd
    state: restarted
```

#### Lets see about using/importing a role from ansible galaxy page


To install a role, you run the below command

```shell
ansible-galaxy role install geerlingguy.docker
```

ansible galaxy can also used for collections.
so once you install this where does the files go ?---> you might seen it in the output lol 

```
~/.ansible/roles/
```

this were it goes


okay now how do you use it in your play book

simple just give the name of it  (name == rolenamespace.actual_role_name)


```yaml
- hosts: webservers
  become: y
  roles:
    - geerlingguy.docker
```


#### Collection

This kind of like roles but for calling apis instead of accessing linux/windows commands

Lets test it with aws

```shell
ansible-galaxy collection install amazon.aws
```

you might have this already installed 

we also need boto3 which is the Amazon SDK this is a dependency for the AWS Collections
```shell
python3 -m pip install boto3
```


Now that we installed everything we can go ahead and use it but but
this collection is going to need authentication method 
with aws it secret keys right

so now the question is how to add the keys to ansible

we can pass it as variables to the task

but this is insecure

we need a way to securely access it

thats were ansible vlauts come it

with vaults you create a password to encryt and decrypt the vault and the vault itself

```shell
# create a random password-- here is a base64 for example

openssl rand -base64 2048 > vault.pass
```

now that we have the password, let setup a vault file with that password

```shell
ansible-vault create pass.yaml --vault-password-file vault.pass
```

when you run the above command it will open up a editor to add your variables in yaml format

save and close it 

if you view the generate file it will encrypted 

to decrypt it
```shell
ansible-vault decrypt pass.yaml --vault-password-file vault.pass
```
eo encrypt it again
```shell
ansible-vault encrypt pass.yaml --vault-password-file vault.pass
```
now that we have pre-requisistes in place let create a aws resource

```yaml
---

- hosts: localhost
  connection: local  #here it is local cause we executing it in the same machine
  vars_files:
    - pass.yam
  tasks:
    - name : start the ec2 instance
      amazon.aws.ec2_instance_info:
        region: 'us-east-1'
        access_key: "{{aws_access_key}}"
        secret_key: "{{aws_secret_key}}
        instance_ids:
          - i-0efad667e6ea71f22
```

you get the below error if you try to run it the way we ran it before

```shell
ansible-playbook ec2-create.yaml
# [WARNING]: No inventory was parsed, only implicit localhost is available
# [WARNING]: provided hosts list is empty, only localhost is available. Note that the implicit localhost does not match 'all'
# ERROR! Attempting to decrypt but no vault secrets found
```
now how do i pass the password for the vault

same way you do it before

```shell
ansible-playbook ec2-create.yaml --vault-password-file vault.pass
```

now this wont give you any output it just run without any problems lol
this is for collecting details 

we need to improve this playbook and make use of variable to print the details in the console

### Loops

Within playbook task you can use loops 
below is a example to use a variable to loop around
```yaml
#Main playbook
---
- hosts: localhost
  connection: local
  vars_files:
    - pass.yaml
  environment:
    AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID: "{{aws_access_key}}"
    AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY: "{{aws_secret_key}}"
    AWS_DEFAULT_REGION: "us-east-1"
  roles:
    - project
```

```yaml
---
# tasks file for project
- name: start an instance with a public IP address
  amazon.aws.ec2_instance:
    name:  "{{item.env_name}}"
    vpc_subnet_id: subnet-0bd2c4ee2ee4d0762
    instance_type: t2.micro
    security_group: default
    network_interfaces:
      - assign_public_ip: true
    image_id: "{{item.ami_id}}"
    tags:
      Environment: "{{item.env_name}}"
    state: terminated
  loop: "{{ instances }}"
```

the corresponding var is
```yaml
---
# vars file for project
#
instances:
  - ami_id: ami-0b826bb6d96d2afe4
    env_name: dev
  - ami_id: ami-0b826bb6d96d2afe4
    env_name: test
  - ami_id: ami-0b826bb6d96d2afe4
    env_name: prod
```

Note how i configured the AWS secrets
Instead of adding the variables in all the task for the collection i have configured as an environment variable and passed the ansible vault 

you might be wondering if i dont have env name in the option what if i had the below var alone

```yaml
ami_id:
  - ami-0b826bb6d96d2afe4
  - ami-0b826bb6d96d2afe4
  - ami-0b826bb6d96d2afe4
```

how will this work

answer is ansible will only create one single instance because ansible is idempotent meaning if a same state already exist then it wont recreate thus making a differenc in each intance makes the difference and creates new instance for each value when looping

## Now lets see about conditions

in ansible you can use "when" keyword and use variables to determine whether a task could get executed or not

```yaml
- hosts: all
  become: true

  tasks:
    - name: print the facts
      ansible.builtin.debug:
        var: ansible_facts
    - name: stop the server if its ubuntu
      ansible.builtin.command: /sbin/shutdown -t now
      when:
        ansible_facts.os_family == "RedHat"
```